# Git for Windows sets core.autocrlf=true by default, and a repository with no
# .gitattributes lets that decide what a checkout contains. It rewrites every
# file Git considers text on the way out of the index, so a Windows contributor
# gets a working tree this project has never tested: scripts/*.sh with CRLF
# terminators, testdata goldens that no longer equal what a test writes, and
# tool output compared against expectations that differ by an invisible byte.
#
# This branch has already paid for that failure mode twice, in the same shape
# each time -- a carriage return riding along in something that was then
# compared for equality, so a digest did not equal itself and printed
# identically on both sides of the message. Those were tools formatting their
# output; this is the checkout itself, and it reaches everything.
#
# eol=lf rather than text=auto alone: `auto` still defers to core.autocrlf for
# the working tree, which is the setting being defended against. This makes the
# answer a property of the repository instead of a property of whoever cloned
# it.
#
# Nothing tracked here carries a CR today, so this normalises no file and
# changes no checkout on Linux or macOS. It only removes a difference Windows
# would otherwise introduce.
* text=auto eol=lf
